type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" run the left-right planarity test (de","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Fraysseix and Rosenstiehl, in Brandes’s formulation, 2009); ","type":"text"},{"code":"findKuratowskiSubgraph()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" runs","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Boyer and Myrvold’s edge-addition algorithm (2004), whose failure state isolates the witness.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"All are O(n + m), iterative, and deterministic: graphs with the same rows (the same vertices in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the same order, the same edge positions, the same incidence order) get the same embedding and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the same witness.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"text":"Planarity on undirected graphs. Self-loops and parallel edges never change an answer: the tests","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"run on the underlying simple graph, read once through the rows in index space. A directed","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"graph’s planarity is that of ","type":"text"},{"code":"graph.undirected","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph"},"references":{"doc://Planarity/documentation/Planarity":{"url":"\/documentation\/planarity","title":"Planarity","role":"collection","type":"topic","kind":"symbol","abstract":[],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity"},"doc://Planarity/documentation/Planarity/GraphProtocols/Graph/planarEmbedding()":{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A planar embedding of the graph, over every edge position (loops and parallel copies"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"included), or nil when the graph is not planar: the witness of planarity (NetworkX’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"check_planarity","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", Boost’s "},{"code":"embedding","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" output). O(n + m), faces included."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/planarEmbedding()","type":"topic","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"planarEmbedding","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> ","kind":"text"},{"text":"PlanarEmbedding","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:9Planarity15PlanarEmbeddingV"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":">?","kind":"text"}],"role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/planarity\/graphprotocols\/graph\/planarembedding()","title":"planarEmbedding()"},"doc://Planarity/documentation/Planarity/GraphProtocols/Graph/isOuterplanar":{"url":"\/documentation\/planarity\/graphprotocols\/graph\/isouterplanar","abstract":[{"text":"Whether the graph has a planar embedding with every vertex on one face (Mathematica’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"OuterplanarGraphQ","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"): whether the graph plus a new vertex joined to every vertex is planar.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"A simple graph with more than 2n − 3 edges is not. O(n + m).","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","title":"isOuterplanar","identifier":"doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/isOuterplanar","type":"topic","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"var","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"isOuterplanar","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Bool","preciseIdentifier":"s:Sb","kind":"typeIdentifier"}]},"doc://Planarity/documentation/Planarity/GraphProtocols/Graph/isPlanar":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"var"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"isPlanar"},{"kind":"text","text":": "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:Sb","text":"Bool"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/isPlanar","kind":"symbol","title":"isPlanar","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Whether the graph can be drawn in the plane without crossing edges (NetworkX’s "},{"code":"is_planar","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":","},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Boost’s "},{"code":"boyer_myrvold_planarity_test","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"). A simple graph with more than 3n − 6 edges is not,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"which is answered after counting distinct pairs, before any search. O(n + m)."}],"url":"\/documentation\/planarity\/graphprotocols\/graph\/isplanar","type":"topic","role":"symbol"},"doc://Planarity/documentation/Planarity/GraphProtocols/Graph/findKuratowskiSubgraph()":{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A subdivision of K₅ or K₃,₃ made of the graph’s edges, or nil when the graph is planar: the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"witness of non-planarity (Boost’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"kuratowski_subgraph"},{"type":"text","text":", JGraphT’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"getKuratowskiSubdivision()"},{"type":"text","text":"). A dense graph is searched on its first 3n − 5 distinct pairs"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"only, which already hold one. O(n + m)."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/findKuratowskiSubgraph()","type":"topic","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"findKuratowskiSubgraph","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> ","kind":"text"},{"text":"KuratowskiSubgraph","preciseIdentifier":"s:9Planarity18KuratowskiSubgraphV","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":">?","kind":"text"}],"role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/planarity\/graphprotocols\/graph\/findkuratowskisubgraph